/-!
# Notation

This guide introduces quite a bit of "standard" graph theory notation not found in
Mathlib.

| Notation | Description | Definition / Target |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `K[n]` | Complete graph on `Fin n` vertices | `completeGraph (Fin n)` |
| `E[n]` | Empty graph on `Fin n` vertices | `emptyGraph (Fin n)` |
| `C[n]` | Cycle graph on `n` vertices | `cycleGraph n` |
| `K[s, t]` | Complete bipartite graph | `completeBipartiteGraph (Fin s) (Fin t)` |
| `L[G]` | Line graph of a graph `G` | `lineGraph G` |
| `P[n]` | Path graph on `n` vertices | `pathGraph n` |
| `Q[n]` | `n`-dimensional hypercube | `hypercube n` |
| `v[a, b, …]` | Hypercube vertex (`open HypercubeVert`) | `((((), a), b), …)` |
| `G[n]` | `n`-uniform blow-up of a graph `G` | `blowUpN G n` |
| `mμ[G]` | Mycielskian of a graph `G` | `mycielskian G` |
| `v ₗ` | "Left" copy of `v` in the Mycielskian | `mycielskian.left v` |
| `v ᵣ` | "Right" copy of `v` in the Mycielskian | `mycielskian.right v` |
| `mμᵥ^n V` | Vertices of the `n`-th iterated Mycielskian | `MycielskianVert V n` |
| `mμ^n G` | `n`-th iterated Mycielskian of a graph `G` | `iterMycielskian G n` |
| `Kn[n, k]` | Kneser graph on `k`-subsets of `Fin n` | `kneserGraph (Fin n) k` |
| `Σg H` | Disjoint union of a family of graphs `H i` | `SimpleGraph.sigma H` |
| `G ⊗g H` | Tensor product of `G` and `H` | `SimpleGraph.tensorProd G H` |
| `⨂g i, G i` | Tensor product of a family of graphs | `SimpleGraph.piTensorProd G` |
| `x ⊕ₕ y` | Componentwise `xor` of hypercube vertices | `HypercubeVert.xor x y` |
| `δ(G)` | Minimum degree of a graph `G` | `SimpleGraph.minDegree G` |
| `Δ(G)` | Maximum degree of a graph `G` | `SimpleGraph.maxDegree G` |
| `τ G` | Vertex cover number of a graph `G` | `SimpleGraph.vertexCoverNum G` |
| `ν G` | Matching number of a graph `G` | `SimpleGraph.matchingNum G` |
| `ω G` | Clique number of a graph `G` | `SimpleGraph.cliqueNum G` |
| `⍺ G` | Independence number of a graph `G` | `SimpleGraph.indepNum G` |
| `χ G` | Chromatic number of a graph `G` | `SimpleGraph.chromaticNumber G` |
| `G⟦v⟧` | Connected component of `G` containing `v` | `connectedComponentMk G v` |
| `λ(G)` | Edge connectivity of a graph `G` | `SimpleGraph.edgeConnectivity G` |
| `κ(G)` | Vertex connectivity of a graph `G` | `SimpleGraph.vertexConnectivity G` |

# Definitions

The following custom definitions and abbreviations are introduced in this guide:

## Chapter 2: SimpleGraph
* `pathNat₁`: path graph on `ℕ` defined using the default `SimpleGraph` constructor.
* `pathNat₂`: path graph on `ℕ` defined using `SimpleGraph.fromRel`.
* `pathNat₃`: path graph on `ℕ` defined using `SimpleGraph.fromEdgeSet`.

*From the exercises:*

* `shiftedPathNat`: graph on `ℕ` with an edge from `n` to `n + 1` for every `n ≥ i`.
* `shortPathNat`: graph on `ℕ` with an edge from `n` to `n + 1` for every `n < i`.

## Chapter 3: Constructions
* `paleyGraph`: Paley graph of a field `F`.
* `cayleyS4`: Cayley graph of the symmetric group on `Fin 4` generated by a 3-cycle
  and a 4-cycle.
* `SimpleGraph.sigma` / `Σg`: disjoint union of an indexed family of graphs.
* `natDisjointComplete`: disjoint union of complete graphs `K[n]`.
* `hypercubeHasse`: Hasse diagram construction of the `n`-dimensional hypercube.
* `hypercubeHamming`: Hamming graph definition of the `n`-dimensional hypercube.
* `HypercubeVert`: recursive definition of the vertex set of the hypercube.
* `hypercube` / `Q[n]`: recursive definition of the `n`-dimensional hypercube.
* `mycielskian` / `mμ[G]`: the Mycielskian of a graph `G`.
* `grotzschGraph`: Grötzsch graph (i.e. `mμ[C[5]]`).
* `mycielskian.left` / `v ₗ`: left copy of a vertex in the Mycielskian.
* `mycielskian.right` / `v ᵣ`: right copy of a vertex in the Mycielskian.
* `MycielskianVert` / `mμᵥ^n V`: vertex type of the `n`-th iterated Mycielskian.
* `iterMycielskian` / `mμ^n G`: `n`-th iterated Mycielskian of `G`.
* `multiNat`: complete multipartite graph with copies of `ℕ` as classes.
* `natFinMulti`: complete multipartite graph with classes `Fin n`.
* `blowUpGraph`: blow-up of a graph `G` by replacing each vertex with a class of
  vertices.
* `blowUpN` / `G[n]`: `n`-uniform blow-up of a graph `G`.
* `kneserGraph` / `Kn[n, k]`: the Kneser graph — vertices are the `k`-element subsets
  of `β` (or of `Fin n`), with edges joining disjoint distinct subsets.
* `petersenGraph`: the Petersen graph, i.e. `Kn[5, 2]`.

## Chapter 4: Decidability, Finiteness and Degrees
* `natNeDec` / `natEvenDec`: `Decidable` instances for `a ≠ b` and `Even m` on `ℕ`,
  synthesized by `inferInstance`, used to inspect which instance Lean picks.
* `myIfThenElse`: branches with `bif` on the Boolean `decide p` from a `Decidable p`
  instance to return a string, mirroring how `ite` is defined.
* `myClassicalIfThenElse`: the same function built with `classical`, hence
  noncomputable.
* `pathNat₂'`: `pathNat₂` restated as an `abbrev`, so that Lean can synthesize
  `DecidableRel` for its adjacency.
* `pathGraphAdjDecidableRw`: deliberately flawed `rw`-based decidability instance,
  illustrating how a non-definitional cast blocks kernel reduction and hence
  `decide`.
* `locallyFiniteOfFintypeEdgeSet`: a graph with finitely many edges is locally
  finite.
* `degIn`: the number of neighbours of `a` lying in a finite set `s`.
* `hypercubeVertToBin`: conversion of a hypercube vertex to its binary string
  representation.
* `degreeMultiset`: the multiset of degrees of all vertices in a finite graph.
* `degreeSequence`: the list of degrees of all vertices in a finite graph, sorted in
  non-increasing order.
* `Graphic`: predicate for a multiset being the degree multiset of some finite simple
  graph.
* `erdosGallaiLE`: the `k`-th Erdős–Gallai inequality for a multiset of degrees.
* `havelHakimiAux`: the Havel–Hakimi test on a non-increasing list, merging rather
  than re-sorting between rounds.
* `havelHakimi`: decides whether a multiset of degrees is graphic via Havel–Hakimi.
* `coneGraph`: adjoins a new vertex to a graph, adjacent to a chosen set of vertices.
* `havelHakimiEdges`: constructive Havel–Hakimi, returning the edge list of a graph
  realizing a labelled graphic degree sequence.
* `graphOfEdgeList`: the graph on `Fin n` with a given list of edges, used to check
  the output of `havelHakimiEdges`.

*From the exercises:*

* `locallyFiniteBlowUpGraph`: a blow-up of a locally finite graph with finite classes
  is locally finite.

## Chapter 5: Maps
* `pathNat₁Translate`: embedding of the infinite path `pathNat₁` into itself.
* `Embedding.blowUp`: canonical embedding of a graph `G` into its blow-up.
* `hypercube.embedBool` / `hypercube.embedFalse` / `hypercube.embedTrue`: embeddings
  of `Q[n]` into `Q[n+1]`.
* `Embedding.mycielskianLeft`: embedding of a graph `G` into its Mycielskian `mμ[G]`.
* `Hom.mapMycielskian`: graph homomorphism `mμ[G] →g mμ[H]` induced by a homomorphism
  `G →g H`.
* `Embedding.mapMycielskian`: graph embedding `mμ[G] ↪g mμ[H]` induced by an
  embedding `G ↪g H`.
* `Iso.mapMycielskian`: graph isomorphism `mμ[G] ≃g mμ[H]` induced by an isomorphism
  `G ≃g H`.
* `tensorProd` / `G ⊗g H`: tensor (categorical) product of two graphs, on the vertex
  type `V × W`.
* `tensorProd.fstHom` / `tensorProd.sndHom`: the two projections out of the tensor
  product as graph homomorphisms.
* `tensorProd.prod`: the homomorphism into `tensorProd G H` induced by a pair of
  homomorphisms (universal property of the product).
* `piTensorProd` / `⨂g i, G i`: tensor product of an arbitrary family of graphs.
* `IsVertexTransitive`: predicate for a graph being vertex-transitive.
* `cycleGraphRotate`: automorphism of `C[n]` rotating the vertices modulo `n`,
  sending `d` to `0`.
* `HypercubeVert.xor` / `⊕ₕ`: bitwise `xor` addition on hypercube vertices.
* `hypercubeTranslate`: automorphism of `Q[n]` by translating by a vertex.
* `isoMycielskianCompleteTwoCycleGraphFive`: the isomorphism `mμ[K[2]] ≃g C[5]`.
* `IndFree`: `A.IndFree B` means that `B` contains no induced copy of `A`.
* `ClawFree`: a graph is *claw-free* if it contains no induced copy of the claw
  `K[1, 3]`.
* `lineGraphCompleteGraphThreeIso`: the isomorphism `L[K[3]] ≃g K[3]`.
* `lineGraphClawIso`: the isomorphism `L[K[1, 3]] ≃g K[3]`.

*From the exercises:*

* `pathGraphEmbedding`: embedding of a path graph `P[n]` into a longer path
  `P[n + k]`.
* `Embedding.mapBlowUp`: embedding of `G` into the blow-up `H.blowUpGraph W` given
  `G ↪g H` and nonempty parts.
* `hypercubeEmbedding`: embedding of `Q[n]` into `Q[n + k]` by iterating
  `hypercube.embedFalse`.
* `hypercubeTwoIsoCycleFour`: the Gray-code isomorphism `Q[2] ≃g C[4]`.

## Chapter 6: Cliques
* `mycielskianProj`: projection from the vertex set of `mμ[G]` to `G` mapping both
  left and right copies to the original vertex.
* `openNeighborhoodSet`: the open neighbourhood of a set `s`, i.e. the union of the
  neighbourhoods of its vertices.
* `openNeighborhoodFinset`: the `Finset` version, for a locally finite graph: the
  vertices adjacent to at least one vertex of `s`.

## Chapter 7: Colorings
* `completeBipartiteGraph.bicoloring`: the natural `Bool`-colouring of
  `completeBipartiteGraph V W` (`true` on the right part); a correctly named alias of
  Mathlib's `CompleteBipartiteGraph.bicoloring`.
* `boxProdColoringLeft` / `boxProdColoringRight`: the colourings of `G` and `H`
  obtained by restricting a colouring of `G □ H` along a fixed vertex of the other
  factor.
* `boxProdColoring`: the `k`-colouring `(v, w) ↦ C₁ v + C₂ w` (addition modulo `k`)
  of `G □ H` built from `k`-colourings of `G` and `H`.
* `HypercubeVert.bool`: the parity (`Bool.xor` of the coordinates) of a hypercube
  vertex, used as a `Bool`-valued colour.
* `hypercube.bicoloring`: the natural `Bool`-colouring of the hypercube `Q[n]`.
* `Coloring.mycielskianLast`: the equivalent `(k + 1)`-colouring of `mμ[G]` in which
  the vertex `none` receives colour `k`.
* `Coloring.ofMycielskian`: the `k`-colouring of `G` induced by a `(k + 1)`-colouring
  of `mμ[G]`.
* `Coloring.mycielskian`: the `(k + 1)`-colouring of `mμ[G]` induced by a
  `k`-colouring of `G`.
* `neighborLTSet`: set of neighbours of a vertex `v` that are smaller than `v`.
* `neighborLTFinset`: finset of neighbours of a vertex `v` that are smaller than `v`.
* `degreeLT`: the number of earlier neighbours of a vertex `v`.
* `LocallyLTFinite`: every vertex has finitely many earlier neighbours (the `LT`
  analogue of `LocallyFinite`).
* `greedyChoice`: the least colour not used by any earlier neighbour of a vertex, via
  `Nat.find`.
* `greedy`: the greedy `ℕ`-colouring of a graph on `ℕ`, defined by well-founded
  recursion.
* `greedyColoringNat`: `greedy` bundled as a `G.Coloring ℕ` for `G : SimpleGraph ℕ`.
* `crownGraph`: the complete bipartite graph on the even and odd naturals minus the
  perfect matching `2n ↦ 2n + 1`.
* `crownGraph.twoColoring`: the natural 2-colouring of `crownGraph` by parity.
* `encodeMap`: a graph on an encodable vertex type transported to `ℕ` via
  `SimpleGraph.map`; its adjacency involves existential quantifiers.
* `SimpleGraph.encode`: a graph on an encodable vertex type transported to a graph on
  `ℕ` via `Encodable.decode₂`, preserving decidability of adjacency.
* `Embedding.encode`: the graph embedding of `G` into `G.encode`.
* `greedyColoringFin`: the greedy `Fin (Δ + 1)`-colouring of a locally finite graph
  with all degrees at most `Δ` on an encodable vertex type.
* `greedyTraced`: a copy of `greedy` that `dbg_trace`s a `*` on every call, used to
  count recursive calls.
* `withTraceCount`: runs a computation with its output captured, returning the value
  together with the number of trace lines.
* `greedyChoiceMin`: the greedy choice computed as a `Finset.min'` over the finitely
  many candidate colours `{0, ..., k}`, where `k` is the number of colours used by
  earlier neighbours.
* `greedyList`: the list of the first `n` greedy colours, built by structural
  recursion so that each colour is computed once.
* `greedyMemo`: the memoized greedy colouring, reading the last entry of
  `greedyList`; `greedy` is compiled to it via `@[csimp]`.
* `greedyColoring`: the greedy `ℕ`-colouring of a graph on an encodable vertex type,
  colouring each vertex by the `greedy` colour of its encoding.

*From the exercises:*

* `kneserColoring`: the explicit `(n - 2 * k + 2)`-colouring of the Kneser graph
  `Kn[n, k]` by least element (exercise).
* `starNat`: the infinite star on `ℕ`, in which `0` is adjacent to every other vertex
  (exercise).

## Chapter 8: Walks
* `Walk.pathNat₁Walk`: walk on the infinite path from `i` to `i + j`.
* `Walk.pathGraphWalk`: walk along the finite path graph `P[n + 1]` from `0` to
  `Fin.last n`.
* `Walk.cycleGraphCycle`: walk once around `C[n + 3]`, built by transferring
  `pathGraphWalk` and closing it with the wrap-around edge.
* `HypercubeVert.hammingPath`: path between two hypercube vertices.
* `HypercubeVert.hammingDist`: Hamming distance between two hypercube vertices
  (number of differing coordinates).
* `HypercubeVert.grayStart` / `HypercubeVert.grayEnd`: start and end vertices of the
  Gray code walk.
* `Walk.grayWalk`: Gray code walk on `Q[n]`.
* `Walk.mycielskianAux`: auxiliary zig-zag walk in `mμ[G]`.
* `Walk.mycielskian`: walk in `mμ[G]` constructed from a walk in `G` (`protected`, to
  coexist with the graph constructor `mycielskian`).
* `Walk.mycielskianClosed`: cycle in `mμ[G]` constructed from a cycle in `G`.
* `Walk.posaRotate`: Pósa rotation of a path walk.
* `Walk.posaClosed`: closing a Pósa path to obtain a cycle.
* `IsClosed`: a *closed* graph — one with no eligible pair, i.e. any two distinct
  vertices with degree sum at least `|V|` are adjacent.
* `modAux`: fuel-limited repeated subtraction of `b` from `a`: a toy example of the
  fuel idiom.
* `closureStep`: one round of the closure process — join every eligible pair at once.
* `closureAux`: fuel-limited iteration of `closureStep`.
* `closure`: the Bondy–Chvátal closure of a graph (computable).
* `IsOrePair`: Ore's condition at a pair of vertices — if distinct and non-adjacent,
  their degree sum is at least `|V|`.
* `IsOreGraph`: an *Ore graph* — every pair of vertices satisfies Ore's condition
  `IsOrePair`.
* `Walk.IsMaximalPath`: a *maximal* path — a path that cannot be extended at either
  end, i.e. every neighbour of either endpoint already lies on it.

*From the exercises:*

* `Walk.grayCycle`: Gray code Hamiltonian cycle in `Q[n + 2]`.
* `chordedC5`: `C[5]` with the two chords `0 — 2` and `0 — 3`, a graph that is not
  an Ore graph but whose closure is complete.
* `Walk.IsMaximumPath`: a *maximum-length* path — a path such that no path in `G` is
  longer.

## Chapter 9: Subgraphs
* `Subgraph.mycielskianSubgraph`: lift of a subgraph in `G` to a subgraph in `mμ[G]`.
* `matchingNum` / `ν G`: matching number of `G` (in `ℕ∞`, mirroring
  `SimpleGraph.vertexCoverNum`), the maximum number of edges in a matching of `G`.
* `Subgraph.boxProdLeft`: a subgraph `M` of `G` copied into every `H`-fibre of
  `G □ H`.
* `hypercube.matching`: perfect matching of `Q[n + 1]`, defined recursively as `⊤` on
  `Q[1]` and the fibre-copy of the previous matching thereafter.
* `Subgraph.mycielskianDouble`: the subgraph of `mμ[G]` with edges `x ₗ — y ᵣ` and
  `x ᵣ — y ₗ` for each edge `x — y` of a subgraph of `G`.
* `Subgraph.mycielskianDoubleDartEmbedding`: embedding of the darts of a subgraph
  into the edge set of its Mycielskian double.
* `ColoringOn`: proper colouring of a subgraph induced by a subset of vertices.
* `ColorableOn`: predicate for a subset of vertices being colourable.
* `coloringOnEmpty`: the colouring of the empty set of vertices by a default colour.
* `ColoringOn.restrict`: restriction of a `G.ColoringOn γ s` to any `t ⊆ s`.
* `ColoringOn.copy`: transport a `G.ColoringOn γ s` along a proof that `s = t`.
* `ColoringOn.toColoring`: a `G.ColoringOn γ Set.univ` is a `G.Coloring γ`.
* `Coloring.toColoringOn`: the `ColoringOn` of a set `s` induced by a colouring of
  the whole graph.
* `coloringOnOfSingleton`: colouring of `{a}` by any colour `c`.
* `coloringOnOfNotAdj`: colouring of `{a, b}` by a single colour `c` when
  `¬ G.Adj a b`.
* `ColoringOn.union`: combination of compatible colourings of `s` and `t` into a
  colouring of `s ∪ t`.
* `ColoringOn.insert`: extension of a colouring of `s` to `insert a s` using a colour
  not used on neighbours of `a` in `s`.
* `ColoringOn.map`: relabelling of the colours of a partial colouring along an
  injective map.

## Chapter 10: Connectivity
* `isoSigmaConnectedComponents`: the isomorphism between `G` and the sigma graph of
  its connected components.
* `EdgesInOneComponent`: every edge of `G` lies in a single connected component (any
  two non-isolated vertices are reachable from each other).
* `locallyFiniteDeleteEdges`: the graph `G.deleteEdges s` obtained by deleting edges
  from a locally finite graph is locally finite.
* `IsVertexConnected`: predicate for a graph being `k`-vertex-connected.
* `Finset.fiberProj`: the vertices `z` of `G` whose copy `(z, b)` in fibre `b` of
  `G □ K₂` lies in a given vertex set.
* `edgeConnectivity` / `λ(G)`: the edge connectivity
  `λ(G) : ℕ∞`, the supremum of the `k` for which `G` is
  `k`-edge-connected.
* `vertexConnectivity` / `κ(G)`: the vertex connectivity
  `κ(G) : ℕ∞`, the supremum of the `k` for which `G` is
  `k`-vertex-connected.
* `walkComponent`: the set of vertices of a finite set `W` reachable from `v₂` by a
  walk lying inside `W`.
* `IsCutVertex`: `x` is a cut vertex of `G` (some two vertices other than `x` are
  joined by a walk, but every walk between them passes through `x`).
* `CycleEquivalent`: two edges are cycle-equivalent if they are equal or lie on a
  common cycle.
* `BlockEquivalent`: the equivalence relation on edges generated by
  `CycleEquivalent`.
* `blockOf`: the subgraph spanned by all edges block-equivalent to a given edge.
* `blockSetoid`: the setoid instance on `G.edgeSet` given by block-equivalence.
* `Block`: a block of `G`, i.e. a block-equivalence class of edges.
* `blockMk`: the block containing a given edge.
* `Block.toGraph`: the subgraph underlying a block.
* `blockOfHom`: an injective homomorphism maps the block of `e` into the block of
  `e.map f`.
* `SplitsAt`: `G` is the union of two proper subgraphs whose supports meet in at most
  a given vertex.

*From the exercises:*

* `botComponentEquiv`: equivalence between the connected components of the empty
  graph and its vertex type.
* `Hom.sigmaDesc`: the universal property of the disjoint union: a family of
  homomorphisms `G i →g H` gives a homomorphism `Σg G →g H`.
* `bowtie`: two triangles sharing a vertex, a graph with `κ(G) < λ(G)`.

## Chapter 11: Distance
* `IsMatchingAndIsolated`: every vertex has at most one neighbour, i.e. the graph is
  a matching together with isolated vertices.
* `mycielskianFourCycle`: 4-cycle in `mμ[G]` using `none` and left/right copies.
* `layer`: the `i`-th distance layer around a vertex `r`, i.e. the `Finset` of
  vertices at distance exactly `i` from `r`.

## Chapter 12: Algebraic Graph Theory
* `Iso.autCongr`: transport of the automorphism group of a graph along an
  isomorphism.
* `cycleGraphReflect`: automorphism of `C[n]` reflecting the vertices (`k ↦ -k`).
* `autCycleGraphMulEquivDihedral`: the automorphism group of `C[n + 3]` is isomorphic
  to `DihedralGroup (n + 3)`.
* `autHypercubeTwoMulEquivDihedral`: the automorphism group of `Q[2]` is isomorphic
  to `DihedralGroup 4`.
* `HypercubeVert.equivPi`: equivalence between the hypercube vertex types
  `HypercubeVert n` and `Fin n → Bool`, via `Fin.snoc`.
* `hypercubeIsoHamming`: the hypercube `Q[n]` is isomorphic to the Hamming graph
  `hypercubeHamming n`.
* `hammingTranslate` / `hammingPermute`: translation by a fixed vector (pointwise
  `xor`) and coordinate permutation as automorphisms of the Hamming graph.
* `hammingPhi`: the action of `Equiv.Perm (Fin n)` on translation vectors by
  permuting coordinates.
* `hyperoctahedralGroup`: the hyperoctahedral group `(ℤ/2)ⁿ ⋊ Sₙ`, i.e. the wreath
  product `ℤ/2 ≀ Sₙ`, as a semidirect product.
* `hammingTranslateHom` / `hammingPermuteHom`: translations and coordinate
  permutations as monoid homs into the automorphism group of the Hamming graph.
* `hyperoctahedralToAut`: the homomorphism from the hyperoctahedral group to the
  automorphisms of the Hamming graph.
* `hyperoctahedralMulEquivAutHamming`: the automorphism group of the Hamming graph is
  the hyperoctahedral group.
* `autHypercubeMulEquivHyperoctahedral`: the automorphism group of `Q[n]` is the
  hyperoctahedral group `ℤ/2 ≀ Sₙ`.
* `chosenSubgraphIso`: a choice, for each subgraph `C` of `B` isomorphic to `A`, of a
  reference isomorphism `A ≃g C.coe`.
* `subgraphIsoEquivAut`: equivalence between the isomorphisms `A ≃g C.coe` of a
  subgraph `C` isomorphic to `A` and the automorphisms of `A`.
* `copyEquivSigmaIso`: equivalence between `A.Copy B` and the Sigma type of subgraphs
  of `B` isomorphic to `A` with their isomorphisms.
* `copyEquivSubgraphIsoProdAut`: equivalence
  `A.Copy B ≃ {C : B.Subgraph | Nonempty (A ≃g C.coe)} × (A ≃g A)`.
* `Iso.copyEquivIso`: for finite `A`, an isomorphism `A ≃g B` identifies copies of
  `A` in `B` with isomorphisms `A ≃g B`.
* `copyEquivAut`: copies of a finite graph in itself are equivalent to its
  automorphisms.
* `copyEquivSubgraphIsoProdCopy`: `A.Copy B` is equivalent to the subgraphs of `B`
  isomorphic to `A` paired with copies of `A` in itself.
* `Iso.copyCongr`: isomorphisms `A ≃g A'` and `B ≃g B'` identify the copies of `A`
  in `B` with the copies of `A'` in `B'`.
* `mycielskianCopyC5OfNeighbors`: the copy of `C[5]` in `mμ[G]` through
  `u ₗ, v ₗ, y ᵣ, none, x ᵣ` built from an edge `u — v` and neighbours `x` of `u` and
  `y` of `v` with `x ≠ y`.
* `copyEquivEmbedding`: if every copy of `A` in `B` reflects adjacency, then copies
  of `A` in `B` are the same as embeddings `A ↪g B`.
* `extendEmb` / `extendHom`: extension of a `k`-vector to an `n`-vector along an
  embedding of directions `Fin k ↪ Fin n`, and the induced homomorphism of Hamming
  graphs.
* `subCubeCopy`: the canonical copy of `Q[k]` in `Q[n]` with a given base point and
  set of free directions.
* `copyEquivProd`: the copies of `Q[k]` in `Q[n]` correspond to pairs of a base point
  and a direction embedding.

*From the exercises:*

* `autMulLeftCayley`: left multiplication by `g` as an automorphism of the Cayley
  graph `mulCayley s`.
* `autTopMulEquivPerm`: the automorphism group of a complete graph is the symmetric
  group.
* `autComplMulEquiv`: a graph and its complement have the same automorphism group.
* `boolVecEquivFinset`: Bool vectors of length `n` correspond to subsets of `Fin n`.
* `hypercubeHammingIsoHasse`: the Hamming graph is isomorphic to the Hasse diagram
  `hypercubeHasse n`.
* `copyTopEquiv`: copies of a graph in a complete graph are the injective maps of
  vertices.

## Chapter 13: Theorems
* `IsNCliqueMaximal`: `G` is `s`-clique-maximal, that is, `(r + 1)`-clique-free and
  with the most `s`-cliques among such graphs on its vertices.
* `cliqueUnion`: the union of the `s`-cliques of `G`; distinct vertices are adjacent
  when they lie in a common `s`-clique.
* `IsCompleteMultipartite.finpartition`: the parts of a finite complete multipartite
  graph, as a `Finpartition` of its vertices.
* `IsDirection`: a direction of `G`, a consistent choice of a component of `G - K`
  for every finite set `K` of vertices (the component for `L` lies inside the one
  for `K` whenever `K ⊆ L`).

# Results

The following key results, theorems, and lemmas are proven throughout this guide and
its exercises:

## Chapter 3: Constructions (Results)
*From the exercises:*

* `paleyGraph₁₇_eq_circulant`: the Paley graph on 17 vertices is equal to the
  circulant graph with differences `{1, 2, 4, 8, 9, 13, 15, 16}`.

## Chapter 4: Decidability, Finiteness and Degrees (Results)
* `good_card_edgeFinset`: the number of edges of `G` depends only on
  `[Fintype G.edgeSet]`; contrast `bad_card_edgeFinset`, which needlessly assumes
  `[Fintype V]` and `[DecidableRel G.Adj]`.
* `completeBipartiteGraph.degree_inl` / `completeBipartiteGraph.degree_inr`:
  vertices in the two parts of `K[s, t]` have degree `t` and `s`.
* `completeBipartiteGraph.card_edgeFinset`: the complete bipartite graph `K[s, t]`
  has `s * t` edges.
* `degree_pathNat₁`: degrees in the infinite path graph on `ℕ`, which is locally
  finite without having a finite vertex type.
* `degree_blowUpN` / `IsRegularOfDegree.blowUpN`: each vertex of the `n`-uniform
  blow-up `G[n]` has degree `n` times its degree in `G`, so the blow-up of a
  `d`-regular graph is `d * n`-regular.
* `isRegularOfDegree_card_sub_one_iff_eq_top`: a graph on a finite vertex type is
  regular of degree `|V| - 1` iff it is complete.
* `sum_degrees_eq_twice_card_edges'`: the handshake lemma with minimal instance
  arguments, dropping the `[DecidableRel G.Adj]` of the Mathlib version; the
  `Fintype` diamond this exposes is closed by `Subsingleton.elim`.
* `IsRegularOfDegree.two_mul_card_edgeFinset` / `IsRegularOfDegree.card_edgeFinset`:
  a `d`-regular graph on `n` vertices has `d * n / 2` edges.
* `card_hypercubeVert`: the hypercube `Q[n]` has `2 ^ n` vertices.
* `hypercube_isRegularOfDegree`: the hypercube `Q[n]` is `n`-regular.
* `card_edgeFinset_hypercube`: the hypercube `Q[n]` has `n * 2 ^ (n - 1)` edges.
* `degree_mycielskian_left`: the degree of a left vertex `v ₗ` of the Mycielskian is
  twice the degree of `v`.
* `graphic_degreeMultiset` / `graphic_degreeSequence`: the degree multiset, and
  degree sequence, of a finite simple graph are graphic.
* `coneGraph_degree_none`: the new vertex of `coneGraph H s` has degree `s.card`, a
  key ingredient of the Havel–Hakimi theorem.
* `erdos_gallai` / `havel_hakimi`: the two characterizations of graphic multisets.
  Both are *stated but not proved* (each is a `sorry`); `havel_hakimi` is used only
  to give a `DecidablePred Graphic` instance that `#eval!` can run.

*From the exercises:*

* `degree_mycielskian_right` / `degree_mycielskian_none`: a right vertex `v ᵣ` of the
  Mycielskian has degree one more than `v`, and the centre vertex has degree `|V|`.
* `card_edgeFinset_mycielskian`: the Mycielskian `mμ[G]` has `3 * |E| + |V|`
  edges.
* `maxDegree_mycielskian` / `minDegree_mycielskian`: the maximum and minimum degrees
  of `mμ[G]` are `max (2 * Δ(G)) (Fintype.card V)` and `min (2 * δ(G)) (δ(G) + 1)`.
* `Graphic.even_sum`: a graphic multiset has an even sum.
* `coneGraph_degree_some` / `coneGraph_degreeMultiset`: the remaining degrees of
  `coneGraph H s`, and its degree multiset.

## Chapter 5: Maps (Results)
* `degree_tensorProd`: the degree of `(v, w)` in the tensor product is
  `G.degree v * H.degree w`.
* `isRegular_of_isVertexTransitive`: a locally finite vertex-transitive graph is
  regular.
* `isVertexTransitive_top`: the complete graph is vertex-transitive.
* `isVertexTransitive_cycleGraph`: the cycle graph `C[n]` is vertex-transitive.
* `isVertexTransitive_hypercube`: the hypercube `Q[n]` is vertex-transitive.
* `isVertexTransitive_mycielskian_iff`: for finite nonempty `V`, the Mycielskian
  `mμ[G]` is vertex-transitive iff `G ≃g K[2]`.
* `clawFree_lineGraph`: line graphs are claw-free.
* `top_three_isContained_lineGraph_iff`: the line graph of `G` contains a triangle
  iff `G` contains a triangle or a (not necessarily induced) copy of the claw.

*From the exercises:*

* `IsVertexTransitive.compl` / `isVertexTransitive_bot`: the complement of a
  vertex-transitive graph is vertex-transitive, so `⊥` is vertex-transitive.

## Chapter 6: Cliques (Results)
* `Hom.cliqueNum_le`: a homomorphism into a finite graph cannot decrease the clique
  number.
* `Iso.cliqueNum_eq`: isomorphic graphs have the same clique number (no finiteness
  needed).
* `CliqueFree.cliqueNum_le`: an `(n + 1)`-clique-free graph has clique number at most
  `n`.
* `degree_le_indepNum_of_triangleFree`: in a finite triangle-free graph every vertex
  has degree at most `⍺ G`.
* `indepNum_kneserGraph`: the independence number of `Kn[n, k]` (for `k ≠ 0`) is
  `(n - 1).choose (k - 1)` if `2 * k ≤ n` and `n.choose k` otherwise, via the
  Erdős–Ko–Rado theorem.
* `CliqueFree.mycielskian`: the Mycielskian of a triangle-free graph is
  triangle-free.
* `IsNClique.mycielskian`: the Mycielskian embedding maps `k`-cliques in `G` to
  `k`-cliques in `mμ[G]`.
* `mycielskian_none_notMem_clique`: the centre vertex `none` does not belong to any
  `k`-clique in `mμ[G]` for `k ≥ 3`.
* `IsNClique.of_mycielskian`: any `k`-clique in `mμ[G]` (with `k ≥ 3`) projects to
  a `k`-clique in `G` under the Mycielskian projection.
* `cliqueNum_mycielskian_of_ne_bot`: the clique number of `mμ[G]` equals that of `G`
  whenever `G` is finite and has an edge.
* `cliqueNum_iterMycielskian_cycleGraph`: every iterated Mycielskian of `C[5]` has
  clique number `2`.
* `IsIndepSet.mycielskian`: if `s` is an independent set of `G` then the left copies
  of `s` together with the right copies of every vertex having no neighbour in `s`
  form an independent set of `mμ[G]`.
* `indepNum_mycielskian`: the independence number of `mμ[G]` is the maximum, over
  the independent sets `s` of `G`, of `#s` plus the number of vertices with no
  neighbour in `s`.

*From the exercises:*

* `cliqueNum_mono`: the clique number is monotone in the subgraph order.
* `cliqueNum_add_indepNum_le`: `ω G + ⍺ G ≤ |V| + 1` for a finite
  graph.
* `not_cliqueFree_three_and_indepSetFree_three`: every graph on at least `6` vertices
  contains a triangle or an independent set of size `3`, i.e. `R(3, 3) ≤ 6`.
* `cliqueNum_mycielskian_bot_of_isEmpty` / `cliqueNum_mycielskian_bot`: the clique
  number of `mμ[⊥]` is `1` if `V` is empty and `2` otherwise.

## Chapter 7: Colorings (Results)
* `chromaticNumber_ne_top_of_finite`: a graph on a finite vertex type has finite
  chromatic number.
* `Colorable.natCard_le_mul_indepNum` / `natCard_le_chromaticNumber_mul_indepNum`:
  a graph has at most `χ G * ⍺ G` vertices, since colour classes are
  independent (trivially if `V` is infinite, when `Nat.card V = 0`).
* `Colorable.sup_of_isClique_inter` / `Colorable.sup_of_subsingleton_inter`:
  colourings of `G₁` and `G₂` from a common palette amalgamate to a colouring of
  `G₁ ⊔ G₂` when the supports meet in a clique (in particular in at most one vertex).
* `blowUpGraph_colorable_iff`: a blow-up of `G` with nonempty parts is `k`-colourable
  iff `G` is.
* `Colorable.boxProd` / `colorable_boxProd_iff` / `chromaticNumber_boxProd`:
  `G □ H` is `k`-colourable when both factors are (iff, for nonempty `G` and
  `H`), and `χ (G □ H) = max (χ G) (χ H)` for nonempty `G` and `H`.
* `hypercube_isBipartite` / `hypercube_cliqueFree_three`: the hypercube `Q[n]` is
  bipartite, hence triangle-free.
* `colorable_mycielskian_succ_iff`: `G` is `k`-colourable iff `mμ[G]` is
  `(k + 1)`-colourable.
* `chromaticNumber_mycielskian`: `χ mμ[G] = χ G + 1`.
* `chromaticNumber_iterMycielskian` / `chromaticNumber_iterMycielskian_C5`: the
  `n`-th iterated Mycielskian of `G` has chromatic number `χ G + n`, so that of
  `C[5]` has chromatic number `n + 3`.
* `degree_le_chromaticNumber_lineGraph` / `maxDegree_le_chromaticNumber_lineGraph`:
  the chromatic index `χ L[G]` is at least the degree of every vertex, and so at
  least `Δ(G)` for a finite graph.
* `greedy_lt` / `Adj.greedy_ne`: the greedy colour of `v` is at most `degreeLT v`,
  and adjacent vertices receive different greedy colours.
* `colorable_of_degreeLT_le`: *the greedy bound* for graphs on `ℕ`: if every vertex
  has at most `Δ` earlier neighbours then the graph is
  `(Δ + 1)`-colourable.
* `crownGraph_neighborLTFinset` / `greedy_crownGraph_eq`: in `crownGraph` the earlier
  neighbours of `n` are the `n / 2` vertices of the other parity below `n`, and the
  greedy colouring colours `n` with `n / 2`, the worst possible.
* `encode_degreeLT_le`: `degreeLT` of an encoded vertex is at most the degree of the
  original vertex.
* `colorable_of_degree_le_of_countable`: a locally finite graph on a countable vertex
  type with all degrees at most `Δ` is `(Δ + 1)`-colourable.
* `greedyChoice_eq_greedyChoiceMin` / `greedyMemo_eq_greedy` /
  `greedy_eq_greedyMemo`: the `Nat.find`, `Finset.min'` and memoized greedy
  colourings agree; the last is the `@[csimp]` lemma that makes `#eval greedy` run
  the memoized version.

*From the exercises:*

* `le_mul_of_colorable_kneserGraph`: if the Kneser graph `Kn[n, k]` is
  `m`-colourable then `n ≤ k * m`, so `χ Kn[n, k] ≥ n / k`.

## Chapter 8: Walks (Results)
* `Walk.IsEulerian.even_degree_iff'`: Mathlib's `even_degree_iff` (in a graph with an
  Eulerian trail every vertex has even degree, except the endpoints of a non-closed
  trail) without `Fintype V`, needing only a finite neighbour set.
* `Walk.cycleGraphCycle_isHamiltonianCycle`: the walk once around `C[n + 3]` is a
  Hamiltonian cycle.
* `cycleGraph_isHamiltonian`: the cycle graph `C[n + 3]` is Hamiltonian.
* `top_isHamiltonian`: the complete graph on at least three vertices is Hamiltonian.
* `HypercubeVert.hammingPath_isPath` / `HypercubeVert.length_hammingPath`: the
  Hamming walk between any two vertices in `Q[n]` is a path of length equal to their
  Hamming distance.
* `Walk.grayWalk_isHamiltonian`: the Gray code walk on `Q[n]` is Hamiltonian.
* `isCycle_mycielskianClosed_of_adj`: every edge of `G` gives a 5-cycle in `mμ[G]`.
* `IsHamiltonian.mycielskian`: if `G` is Hamiltonian, then its Mycielskian `mμ[G]` is
  Hamiltonian.
* `Walk.IsPath.posaRotate` / `Walk.IsPath.posaClosed_isCycle`: a Pósa rotation of a
  path is again a path; closing a path of length at least two at a crossing pair
  yields a cycle.
* `Walk.IsMaximalPath.exists_crossing_index`: pigeonhole counting — a maximal path
  whose endpoints have degree sum at least `|V|` has a *crossing pair*.
* `Walk.IsMaximalPath.exists_cycle_spanning`: a maximal path of length at least two
  whose endpoints form an `IsOrePair` can be closed (by `concat` or `posaClosed`)
  into a cycle through exactly its vertices.
* `Walk.IsHamiltonianCycle.exists_isHamiltonian_walk_of_mem_edges`: a Hamiltonian
  cycle through an edge `u — v` can be cut open into a Hamiltonian walk from `u` to
  `v` avoiding `u — v`.
* `sup_edge_isHamiltonian_iff`: the Bondy–Chvátal one-step lemma — joining a
  non-adjacent pair with degree sum at least `|V|` does not change whether the graph
  is Hamiltonian.
* `closureStep_isHamiltonian_iff`: a round of the closure process does not change
  whether the graph is Hamiltonian.
* `closure_isHamiltonian_iff`: the Bondy–Chvátal theorem — a graph is Hamiltonian iff
  its closure is.
* `isClosed_closure`: the closure is closed — no eligible pair remains in it.
* `closure_min`: the closure is below every closed graph above `G`, hence is the
  *least* closed supergraph — independent of the order in which eligible pairs are
  joined.
* `closure_mono` / `closure_closure`: monotonicity and idempotence of the closure,
  each a one-line consequence of `closure_min`.
* `isHamiltonian_of_closure_eq_top`: a graph whose closure is complete is
  Hamiltonian.
* `IsOreGraph.mono` / `IsOreGraph.eq_top_of_isClosed`: Ore's condition is inherited
  by supergraphs, and a closed Ore graph is complete.
* `IsOreGraph.closure_eq_top`: the closure of an Ore graph is the complete graph.
* `IsOreGraph.isHamiltonian`: Ore's theorem — a graph on at least three vertices
  satisfying Ore's degree condition is Hamiltonian.
* `isHamiltonian_of_card_le_two_mul_minDegree`: Dirac's theorem — minimum degree at
  least `|V| / 2` implies Hamiltonian.

*From the exercises:*

* `cycleGraph_isHamiltonian_iff` / `completeGraph_isHamiltonian_iff`: `C[n]` and
  `K[n]` are Hamiltonian iff `n ≠ 0` and `n ≠ 2`.
* `HypercubeVert.hammingDist_comm`: the Hamming distance on the hypercube is
  symmetric.
* `Walk.grayCycle_isHamiltonianCycle`: closing the Gray code walk yields a
  Hamiltonian cycle in `Q[n + 2]`.
* `hypercube_isHamiltonian`: the hypercube `Q[n]` is Hamiltonian for `n ≠ 1`.
* `Walk.IsHamiltonian.posaClosed_isHamiltonianCycle`: closing a Hamiltonian path via
  a Pósa rotation yields a Hamiltonian cycle.
* `Walk.IsHamiltonianCycle.reverse`: the reverse of a Hamiltonian cycle is a
  Hamiltonian cycle.
* `Walk.IsMaximumPath.isMaximalPath`: a maximum-length path is in particular a
  maximal path, so the chapter's closing construction applies to it.
* `Walk.isHamiltonian_of_isMaximumPath`: a maximum-length path in an Ore graph is
  Hamiltonian — the classical longest-path proof of Ore's theorem.

## Chapter 9: Subgraphs (Results)
* `colorable_of_forall_finite_subgraph_colorable`: the De Bruijn–Erdős theorem, a
  graph is `n`-colourable if all its finite subgraphs are.
* `colorable_of_degree_le`: a locally finite graph of maximum degree at most `Δ` is
  `(Δ + 1)`-colourable.
* `Subgraph.IsMatching.le_matchingNum` / `matchingNum_le` /
  `exists_isMatching_matchingNum`: the basic API for the matching number `ν G` (lower
  bounds from matchings, upper bounds from uniform bounds, attainment).
* `Subgraph.IsMatching.ncard_inter_le`: in a graph that is bipartite with parts `p₁`
  and `p₂`, a finite matching has at least as many edges as vertices in `p₁`.
* `matchingNum_le_card_div_two` / `Subgraph.IsPerfectMatching.matchingNum_eq`: a
  finite graph has `ν G ≤ |V| / 2`, with equality if it has a perfect matching.
* `matchingNum_le_vertexCoverNum`: weak duality `ν G ≤ τ G`.
* `hypercube.matchingNum_succ`: `ν Q[n + 1] = 2 ^ n`.
* `two_mul_matchingNum_le_matchingNum_mycielskian` /
  `Subgraph.IsPerfectMatching.matchingNum_mycielskian_eq`: `2 * ν G ≤ ν mμ[G]`, with
  equality if `G` has a perfect matching.

*From the exercises:*

* `Subgraph.mem_verts_iff_of_isCompl`: if subgraphs `H` and `K` are complementary
  (`IsCompl H K`) then no edge of `G` has exactly one endpoint in `H`.
* `colorable_of_forall_finite_induce_colorable`: a graph is `n`-colourable if all its
  finite induced subgraphs `G.induce s` are.
* `matchingNum_mono`: the matching number is monotone in the subgraph order.
* `matchingNum_pathNat₁`: the infinite path has matching number `⊤`.
* `indepNum_add_matchingNum_le`: in a finite graph `⍺ G + ν G ≤ |V|`.
* `matchingNum_exists`: the matching number is attained in every graph, finite or
  not; when `ν G = ⊤` a greedy chain of matchings produces an infinite one.
* `colorableOn_iff_colorable_induce`: for `n > 0`, `G.ColorableOn n s` iff
  `G.induce s` is `n`-colourable.

## Chapter 10: Connectivity (Results)
* `Connected.card_connectedComponent`: a connected graph has exactly one connected
  component.
* `hypercube_connected`: the `n`-dimensional hypercube is connected.
* `connected_mycielskian_iff`: the Mycielskian `mμ[G]` is connected iff `G` has no
  isolated vertices.
* `isBridge_mycielskian_iff`: the bridges of `mμ[G]` are the edges `none — u ᵣ` with
  `u` isolated in `G`.
* `pathNat₁_connected` / `pathNat₁_isAcyclic` / `pathNat₁_isTree`: the infinite path
  on `ℕ` is a tree (so every edge is a bridge).
* `Walk.exists_odd_isCycle` / `isBipartite_iff_forall_isCycle_even`: a closed walk of
  odd length contains a cycle of odd length, so a graph is bipartite iff it has no
  odd cycle.
* `Walk.IsCycle.mem_support_of_degree_le_two` /
  `Walk.IsCycle.isHamiltonianCycle_of_degree_le_two`: in a graph of maximum degree at
  most `2`, every cycle passes through every vertex of its component, so in a
  connected such graph every cycle is a Hamiltonian cycle.
* `Walk.exists_isEulerian_circuit`: Euler's Theorem, existence direction (if all
  edges lie in one component and every vertex has even degree, there is an Eulerian
  circuit).
* `Walk.exists_isEulerian_trail_of_two_odd`: if all edges lie in one component and
  exactly two vertices `a ≠ b` have odd degree, there is an Eulerian trail from `a`
  to `b`.
* `hypercube_isEdgeConnected`: the `n`-dimensional hypercube is `n`-edge-connected.
* `hypercube_isVertexConnected`: the `n`-dimensional hypercube is
  `n`-vertex-connected.
* `isVertexConnected_iff_induce`: `G` is `k`-vertex-connected iff it has more than
  `k` vertices and `G.induce Sᶜ` is preconnected whenever `S.encard < k`.
* `vertexConnectivity_le_edgeConnectivity`: `κ(G) ≤ λ(G)` for every
  graph.
* `brooks_two`: Brooks' theorem for `Δ = 2`, a finite graph of maximum degree at
  most `2` is `2`-colourable unless one of its components is an odd cycle.
* `brooks`: Brooks' theorem for `Δ ≥ 3`, a finite graph with maximum degree
  at most `Δ` and no `(Δ + 1)`-clique is `Δ`-colourable.
* `colorable_of_cliqueFree_of_degree_le`: the same for locally finite graphs.
* `brooks_general`: Brooks' theorem for every `Δ`.
* `isVertexConnected_two_iff`: a graph is `2`-vertex-connected iff it has at least
  three vertices, is connected and has no cut vertex.
* `IsCutVertex.splitsAt` / `splitsAt_of_not_reachable`: a graph splits at each of its
  cut vertices, and at every vertex if its edges lie in two different components.
* `colorable_of_forall_block`: for `n ≥ 1`, a finite graph is `n`-colourable if
  each of its blocks is.

*From the exercises:*

* `IsTree.degree_le_card_leaves`: a finite tree has at least as many leaves as the
  degree of any of its vertices.
* `Walk.exists_odd_isCycle_length_le`: a closed walk of odd length `k` contains an
  odd cycle of length at most `k`.
* `IsVertexConnected.le_minDegree`: a `k`-vertex-connected graph has minimum degree
  at least `k`.
* `edgeConnectivity_le_minDegree`: `λ(G) ≤ δ(G)` for a finite graph
  with at least two vertices (completing Whitney's inequalities).
* `hypercube_vertexConnectivity` / `hypercube_edgeConnectivity`: `κ(Q[n]) = n`,
  and `λ(Q[n + 1]) = n + 1` (while `λ(Q[0]) = ⊤`).
* `IsAcyclic.blockOf_eq`: in a forest the block of an edge is that single edge.
* `colorable_iff_forall_block`: for `n ≥ 1`, a graph is `n`-colourable iff each of
  its blocks is.

## Chapter 11: Distance (Results)
* `Iso.edist_eq`: graph isomorphisms preserve the graph distance.
* `edist_eq_hammingDist`: the graph distance between any two vertices in `Q[n]` is
  exactly equal to their Hamming distance.
* `hypercube_adj_iff_hammingDist_eq_one`: adjacency in `Q[n]` is equivalent to
  Hamming distance one.
* `IsAcyclic.edist_eq_length`: in an acyclic graph the distance between the endpoints
  of a path is its length.
* `edist_pathNat₁` / `ediam_pathNat₁` / `diam_pathNat₁`: in the infinite path the
  distance from `m` to `m + k` is `k`, so its diameter is `⊤` and its `ℕ`-valued
  diameter is the junk value `0`.
* `Iso.eccent_eq` / `Iso.ediam_eq` / `Iso.radius_eq`: eccentricity, diameter and
  radius are isomorphism invariants.
* `IsVertexTransitive.eccent_eq` / `IsVertexTransitive.radius_eq_ediam` /
  `IsVertexTransitive.center_eq_univ`: in a vertex-transitive graph all
  eccentricities are equal, the radius equals the diameter and every vertex is
  central.
* `ediam_boxProd`: the diameter of a box product of graphs on nonempty vertex types
  is the sum of the diameters.
* `ediam_hypercube` / `radius_hypercube`: the hypercube `Q[n]` has diameter and
  radius `n`.
* `mycielskian_radius_le_two` / `mycielskian_ediam_le_four`: the Mycielskian of a
  graph without isolated vertices has radius at most `2` and diameter at most `4`.
* `mycielskian_diam_le_four`: the `ℕ`-valued diameter of any Mycielskian is at most
  `4`.
* `le_egirth_of_free`: a graph containing no copy of `C[n]` for `3 ≤ n ≤ k` has
  girth at least `k + 1`.
* `cliqueFree_three_iff_free_cycleGraph`: a graph is triangle-free iff it contains no
  copy of `C[3]`.
* `four_le_egirth_of_cliqueFree_three` / `five_le_egirth`: a triangle-free graph has
  girth at least `4`, and at least `5` if it also has no `4`-cycle.
* `egirth_cycleGraph` / `ediam_cycleGraph`: the cycle graph `C[n + 3]` has girth
  `n + 3` and diameter `(n + 3) / 2`.
* `egirth_completeBipartiteGraph`: the complete bipartite graph `K[s + 2, t + 2]` has
  girth `4`.
* `egirth_hypercube`: the girth of the hypercube `Q[n + 2]` is `4`.
* `egirth_mycielskian_eq_four` / `egirth_mycielskian_eq_five`: for triangle-free `G`
  the girth of `mμ[G]` is `4` if two edges of `G` share an endpoint, and `5` if `G`
  is a matching plus isolated vertices with at least one edge.
* `egirth_le_edist_add_edist_add_two`: two distinct neighbours `a` and `b` of `v`,
  neither further from `r` than `v`, give a cycle of length at most
  `G.edist r a + G.edist r b + 2`.
* `moore_bound` / `moore_bound_of_radius_le`: the Moore bound on the order of a graph
  of maximum degree at most `d + 1` and diameter (or radius) at most `k`.
* `cage_bound_odd` / `cage_bound_even`: the cage bounds on the order of a graph of
  minimum degree at least `δ + 1` and girth at least `2 * k + 1` (resp. `2 * k`).
* `ediam_petersenGraph` / `egirth_petersenGraph`: the Petersen graph has diameter `2`
  and girth `5`, so it attains the Moore bound.
* `Colorable.extend_precoloring`: Albertson's theorem, a precolouring of vertices
  pairwise at distance at least `4` in a `k`-colourable graph extends to a
  `(k + 1)`-colouring.

## Chapter 12: Algebraic Graph Theory (Results)
* `aut_pathNat₁_eq_one` / `card_aut_pathNat₁` / `not_isVertexTransitive_pathNat₁`:
  the infinite path `pathNat₁` is asymmetric, i.e. its only automorphism is the
  identity, so it is not vertex-transitive.
* `aut_cycleGraph`: every automorphism of `C[n + 2]` is a rotation, or a rotation
  followed by the reflection.
* `adjMatrix_submatrix_eq_iff`: a permutation of the vertices is an automorphism iff
  it preserves the adjacency matrix.
* `IsVertexTransitive.adjMatrix_pow_apply_self` /
  `IsVertexTransitive.trace_adjMatrix_pow`: in a vertex-transitive graph every vertex
  lies on the same number of closed walks of each length.
* `IsVertexTransitive.card_walk_self_eq_trace_div`: in a vertex-transitive graph on
  `n` vertices each vertex lies on `tr(Aᵏ) / n` closed walks of length `k`.
* `Iso.charpoly_adjMatrix`: isomorphic graphs have adjacency matrices with the same
  characteristic polynomial.
* `IsRegularOfDegree.finrank_eigenspace_adjMatrix`: in a `d`-regular graph the
  multiplicity of the eigenvalue `d` is the number of connected components.
* `petersenGraph_isSRGWith`: the Petersen graph is strongly regular with parameters
  `(10, 3, 0, 1)`.
* `HypercubeVert.hammingDist_equivPi`: `HypercubeVert.equivPi` identifies
  `HypercubeVert.hammingDist` with Mathlib's `hammingDist`.
* `hypercubeHamming_edist_eq`: the graph distance in the Hamming graph is the Hamming
  distance.
* `hammingDist_aut` / `hammingNorm_aut`: automorphisms of the Hamming graph preserve
  Hamming distance (and weight, if they fix `0`).
* `aut_eq_id_of_fix_zero_and_singles`: an automorphism of the Hamming graph fixing
  `0` and all basis vectors is the identity.
* `card_aut_hypercube`: the hypercube `Q[n]` has exactly `2 ^ n * n.factorial`
  automorphisms.
* `labelledCopyCount_eq_copyCount_mul_labelledCopyCount`: the number of labelled
  copies of a finite graph `A` in `B` is the number of unlabelled copies times the
  number of automorphisms of `A`.
* `labelledCopyCount_cycleGraph`: the number of labelled copies of `C[n + 3]` in a
  graph is `2 * (n + 3)` times the number of unlabelled copies.
* `card_copy_cycleGraph_eq_mul`: if every copy of `C[n]` in `H` passes through `w`,
  there are `n` times as many copies as copies sending a fixed vertex to `w`.
* `labelledCopyCount_mycielskian_cycleGraph_five` /
  `copyCount_mycielskian_cycleGraph_five`: if `G` has no triangles and no 5-cycles,
  the number of 5-cycles in `mμ[G]` is the sum of `G.degree u * G.degree v` over the
  edges `u — v` of `G`.
* `copy_cycleGraph_five_adj_iff` / `card_embedding_mycielskian_cycleGraph_five`:
  every copy of `C[5]` in a triangle-free graph is induced, so the same formula
  counts the induced 5-cycles of `mμ[G]`.
* `labelledCopyCount_hypercube` / `copyCount_hypercube`: the number of labelled
  (resp. unlabelled) copies of `Q[k]` in `Q[n]` is `2 ^ n * n.descFactorial k` (resp.
  `n.choose k * 2 ^ (n - k)`).
* `subCubeCopy_edist`: canonical subcube copies are isometric embeddings.
* `copy_hypercubeHamming_adj_iff` / `card_embedding_hypercubeHamming`: every copy of
  `Q[k]` in `Q[n]` is induced.

*Mathlib theorems surveyed in this chapter:*

* `adjMatrix_pow_apply_eq_card_walk`: the `(u, v)` entry of the `n`-th power of the
  adjacency matrix is the number of walks of length `n` from `u` to `v`.
* `IsRegularOfDegree.mem_spectrum_adjMatrix`: a `d`-regular graph has eigenvalue `d`.
* `card_connectedComponent_eq_finrank_ker_toLin'_lapMatrix`: the number of connected
  components of a finite graph is the dimension of the kernel of its Laplacian.
* `IsSRGWith.param_eq`: the parameters of a strongly regular graph satisfy
  `k * (k - ℓ - 1) = (n - k - 1) * μ`.
* `IsSRGWith.ediam_eq_two`: a strongly regular graph with `μ ≠ 0` that is not
  complete has diameter `2`.

*From the exercises:*

* `isVertexTransitive_mulCayley`: the Cayley graph of a group is vertex-transitive.
* `card_aut_cycleGraph` / `card_aut_completeGraph`: `C[n + 3]` has `2 * (n + 3)`
  automorphisms and `K[n]` has `n.factorial`.
* `even_degree_of_isVertexTransitive`: in a vertex-transitive graph with an odd
  number of vertices every degree is even.
* `isVertexTransitive_kneserGraph`: Kneser graphs, and so the Petersen graph, are
  vertex-transitive.
* `exists_aut_hypercubeHamming_of_adj`: the hypercube is arc-transitive.
* `copyCount_completeGraph_cycleGraph`: `K[n + 3]` has
  `(n + 3).factorial / (2 * (n + 3))` Hamiltonian cycles.
* `copyCount_hypercube_cycleGraph_four`: `Q[n]` contains `n.choose 2 * 2 ^ (n - 2)`
  4-cycles.
* `card_interedges_le_of_card_eq_two_pow`: the edge-isoperimetric inequality, a set
  of `2 ^ k` vertices of `Q[n]` spans at most `k * 2 ^ (k - 1)` edges.
* `exists_card_interedges_eq`: the copies of `Q[k]` in `Q[k + m]` attain the
  edge-isoperimetric inequality.

## Chapter 13: Theorems (Results)
* `exists_isMatching_of_forall_ncard_le'`: Hall's theorem for one side of a
  bipartite graph, without assuming local finiteness.
* `exists_isMatching_of_forall_ncard_le_add`: Hall's theorem with deficiency `d` — if
  every `s ⊆ p₁` has at least `|s| - d` neighbours, a matching covers all but at most
  `d` vertices of `p₁`.
* `IsBipartite.matchingNum_eq_vertexCoverNum`: Kőnig's theorem — `ν G = τ G` for a
  finite bipartite graph.
* `CliqueFree.exists_colorable_two_mul_card_edgeFinset_le`: Füredi's stability
  theorem — an `(r + 1)`-clique-free graph can be made `r`-colourable by deleting at
  most `turanNumber n r - e(G)` edges.
* `card_cliqueFinset_replaceVertex`: giving `b` the neighbours of `a` (the graph
  `G.replaceVertex a b`) removes the `s`-cliques through `b` and adds as many as
  there are `s`-cliques through `a` but not `b`.
* `IsCompleteMultipartite.exists_isNClique_of_le`: in a complete multipartite graph
  with an `s`-clique (`2 ≤ s`), any edge `xy` of a larger graph lies in an `s`-clique
  of that graph.
* `IsCompleteMultipartite.nonempty_iso_turanGraph`: a complete multipartite
  `(r + 1)`-clique-free graph with balanced parts, to which no edge can be added, is
  isomorphic to the Turán graph.
* `IsNCliqueMaximal.nonempty_iso_turanGraph`: an `(r + 1)`-clique-free graph with
  the most `s`-cliques, where `2 ≤ s`, is a Turán graph if it has an `s`-clique at
  all.
* `Iso.card_cliqueFinset_eq`: isomorphic graphs have the same number of `s`-cliques.
* `CliqueFree.card_cliqueFinset_le`: Zykov's theorem — an `(r + 1)`-clique-free graph
  on `n` vertices has at most as many `s`-cliques as `turanGraph n r`.
* `CliqueFree.card_cliqueFinset_eq_iff_nonempty_iso_turanGraph`: uniqueness in
  Zykov's theorem — for `2 ≤ s ≤ r` and `s ≤ n`, an `(r + 1)`-clique-free graph has
  as many `s`-cliques as `turanGraph n r` iff it is isomorphic to it.
* `mem_end_iff`: the ends of `G`, as defined in Mathlib, are exactly its directions.
* `ComponentCompl.mem_of_hom`: the image of a connected graph under a homomorphism
  avoiding `K` lies in a single component of `G - K`.

*Mathlib theorems surveyed in this chapter:*

* `exists_isMatching_of_forall_ncard_le` /
  `exists_isPerfectMatching_of_forall_ncard_le`: Hall's marriage theorem for locally
  finite bipartite graphs, giving a matching that covers one side, or a perfect
  matching.
* `Subgraph.IsPerfectMatching.symmDiff_isCycles`: the symmetric difference of two
  perfect matchings is a disjoint union of cycles.
* `tutte`: Tutte's theorem — a finite graph has a perfect matching iff it has no
  Tutte violator.
* `isTuranMaximal_iff_nonempty_iso_turanGraph`: Turán's theorem — for `0 < r` the
  `K[r + 1]`-free graphs with the most edges are exactly the Turán graphs.
* `CliqueFree.card_edgeFinset_le`: a `K[r + 1]`-free graph on `n` vertices has at
  most `turanNumber n r` edges; with `turanNumber_two` this is Mantel's theorem.
* `IsBipartite.four_mul_encard_edgeSet_le`: a bipartite graph on `n` vertices has
  at most `n ^ 2 / 4` edges, with `ℕ∞`-valued cardinalities so that no finiteness
  assumption is needed.
* `colorable_of_cliqueFree_lt_minDegree`: the Andrásfai–Erdős–Sós theorem — a
  `K[r + 1]`-free graph of large minimum degree is `r`-colourable.
* `extremalNumber_top`: Turán's theorem for extremal numbers — the extremal number
  of a complete graph on `r + 1` vertices is the number of edges of `turanGraph n r`.
* `tendsto_turanDensity` / `isEquivalent_extremalNumber`: the extremal number of `H`
  is asymptotically its Turán density times `n.choose 2`.
* `eventually_completeEquipartiteGraph_isContained_of_minDegree`: the Erdős–Stone
  theorem, in its minimum degree form.
* `szemeredi_regularity`: Szemerédi's regularity lemma.
* `triangle_removal`: the triangle removal lemma.
* `roth_3ap_theorem_nat`: Roth's theorem — a set of natural numbers of positive
  density contains a three-term arithmetic progression.
* `corners_theorem_nat`: the corners theorem — a dense set of points in the grid
  contains a corner `(x, y), (x + d, y), (x, y + d)` with `d ≠ 0`.
* `exists_seq_covby_of_forall_covby_finite`: Kőnig's lemma, in an order-theoretic
  form.
* `nonempty_ends_of_infinite`: an infinite, connected, locally finite graph has at
  least one end.

*From the exercises:*

* `card_cliqueFinset_two`: a graph has as many `2`-cliques as edges, so Zykov's
  theorem for `s = 2` is the edge bound of Turán's theorem.
